    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rashtrakutas were a family of chiefs and officials in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a>, probably Kannada-speaking, who served the Chalukyas of Badami as feudatories in the early eighth century. Their earlier history is obscure, and several separate Rashtrakuta lineages are known from inscriptions. The founder of the imperial line, Dantidurga, built up his power around Ellora and the upper Godavari valley, and his inscriptions claim campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malwa">Malwa</a> and Kosala; his record is carved in the Dashavatara cave at Ellora. The Samangad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a>-plate grant of 753 already shows him claiming paramount titles, and around that year he defeated the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kirtivarman-ii">Kirtivarman II</a>, ending Chalukya supremacy in the western Deccan. Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the Western Ganga dynasty of southern Karnataka and attacked the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi. A grant of 812 praises Krishna I for a marvellous temple at Elapura, generally identified with the Kailasa temple at Ellora, cut downwards into the basalt hillside as a single monolithic shrine. After the short reign of his son Govinda II, the throne passed c. 780 to Govinda’s brother Dhruva Dharavarsha, who turned the kingdom into an empire contending for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gulf-of-khambhat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat.jpg" alt="Gulf of Khambhat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gujarat_Gulfs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rashtrakuta heartland lay on the black-soil plateau of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pulses">pulses</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a> were grown, and cotton textiles were among the region’s best-known products. Most people lived in villages that paid a share of the harvest to the king or to the holders of granted land, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a>-plate charters record gifts of villages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brahmins">Brahmins</a>, temples and Jain monasteries. The empire controlled ports on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and, through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a> branch, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>, where Arab and Persian merchants traded. Arab writers, among them the merchant Sulaiman in 851 and al-Masudi, who visited western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> c. 916, called the Rashtrakuta king the Balhara, a corruption of the title Vallabharaja, and described him as friendly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabs">Arabs</a> and tolerant of Muslim communities in his ports. Religious life was plural: the kings were mostly devotees of Shiva and Vishnu, but Jainism enjoyed generous patronage. Under Amoghavarsha I the Jain teachers Jinasena and Gunabhadra composed the <em>Adipurana</em>, and the mathematician Mahaviracharya wrote the <em>Ganitasarasangraha</em> c. 850. Temples and Brahmin settlements served as centres of learning, such as the college at Salotgi recorded in 945. Merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guild">guilds</a> operated in the towns, and Kannada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> were both used in inscriptions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#govinda-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/govinda-iii.jpg" alt="Govinda III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#govinda-iii">Govinda III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscription_of_Rashtrakuta_Emperor_Govinda_III_at_Veerabhadra_temple,_Mavali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rashtrakuta power rested on large armies of infantry, cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a> raised from the royal domain and from feudatories, and Arab authors were impressed by their size. The dynasty’s wars were dominated by the so-called tripartite struggle with the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty and the Pala Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a> for control of Kannauj and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>. Around 786–790 Dhruva crossed the Narmada and defeated both the Pratihara king Vatsaraja and the Pala king Dharmapala in the Ganga–Yamuna doab. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> repeated the feat c. 800, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a>, while Dharmapala and his client Chakrayudha of Kannauj submitted; Rashtrakuta inscriptions claim that his horses drank from the Himalayan streams. Govinda III also broke a southern coalition of Pallava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>, Kerala and Ganga rulers and took tribute from Kanchipuram. Amoghavarsha I fought a long war against the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi, winning at Vingavalli c. 830. Indra III marched north again and sacked Kannauj c. 916, humbling the Pratihara Mahipala I. The last great commander, Krishna III, allied with the Western Ganga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#butuga-ii">Butuga II</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> at the Battle of Takkolam in 949, where the Chola prince Rajaditya was killed on his elephant, and occupied the Tondaimandalam region around Kanchi for several years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king took the imperial titles <em>maharajadhiraja</em>, <em>paramabhattaraka</em> and <em>parameshvara</em> and ruled with ministers, a crown prince and a court that issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Kannada <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a>-plate grants recording the royal genealogy and the transfer of land. Succession was frequently contested between brothers: Dhruva displaced Govinda II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#govinda-iii">Govinda III</a> had to defeat his brother Stambha, and Govinda IV was overthrown in a court revolt in 936. Under the king, the territory was divided into provinces called <em>rashtra</em> under a governor, districts called <em>vishaya</em>, smaller units called <em>bhukti</em>, and villages run by hereditary headmen with local assemblies. Much of the empire, however, was held by feudatories who paid tribute and supplied troops, including the Western Gangas, the Chalukyas of Vemulavada, the Silaharas of the Konkan and, for a time, the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malwa">Malwa</a>. A collateral branch descended from Govinda III’s brother Indra governed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a> from c. 800. The capital moved several times: the early kings ruled from the Ellora region, Govinda III from Mayurakhandi, and Amoghavarsha I founded Manyakheta, modern Malkhed in Kalaburagi district, which remained the seat of government until 972. Amoghavarsha’s long reign from 814 to 878 was noted for internal peace, generous religious patronage and the king’s personal interest in literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#srivijaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srivijaya.jpg" alt="Srivijaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#srivijaya">Srivijaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srivijaya_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunawan Kartapranata, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than two centuries the Rashtrakutas were the leading power of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and one of the three great states of the subcontinent, and their campaigns reached both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and the far south. Their reputation travelled abroad: Sulaiman’s account of 851 counted the Balhara among the four great kings of the world, alongside the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>, the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and the Byzantine emperor. The Kailasa temple at Ellora, together with the Jain caves of the same site, is among the most ambitious works of rock-cut architecture in the world and part of the Ellora Caves World Heritage Site. The period was formative for Kannada literature. The <em>Kavirajamarga</em>, a treatise on poetics attributed to Amoghavarsha I or his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, is the earliest surviving literary work in Kannada, and in the tenth century the poets Pampa, author of the <em>Vikramarjuna Vijaya</em> and a Jain <em>Adipurana</em> completed in 941, and Ponna worked under the Rashtrakutas and their feudatories. Their patronage of Jain scholarship made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> a major centre of Digambara learning. Politically, the empire’s feudatories became its heirs: the Western Chalukyas, the Silaharas and the Paramaras all rose from its framework, and the Western Chalukya state largely took over its territory and administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tailapa-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii.jpg" alt="Tailapa II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_913_(c.991_AD)_of_Kalyani_(Western)_Chalukya_King_Ahvamalla_Tailapa_II.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jugaari cross, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire remained formidable under Krishna III, but its strength depended on his personal leadership and on loyal feudatories. After his death in 967 his brother Khottiga faced the Paramara ruler Siyaka II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malwa">Malwa</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a>, who invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> and sacked Manyakheta in 972, an event recorded by the poet Dhanapala. The blow exposed the weakness of the throne. In 973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a>, a feudatory based in the Bijapur region who claimed descent from the Chalukyas of Badami, overthrew Khottiga’s nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karka-ii">Karka II</a> and proclaimed the Western Chalukya Empire, soon making himself master of most of the old Rashtrakuta lands. Resistance continued in the south, where the Western Ganga king Marasimha II and his general Chavundaraya backed Indra IV, a grandson of Krishna III, as Rashtrakuta claimant. The cause collapsed after Marasimha’s death, and Indra IV died in 982 at the Jain centre of Shravanabelagola by the ritual fast of <em>sallekhana</em>, ending the imperial line. Minor Rashtrakuta families persisted as local chiefs, and some later dynasties of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> claimed descent from them, though these links are uncertain. The Western Chalukyas inherited the empire’s position in the Deccan, continuing its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chola-empire">Cholas</a> for another two centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Rashtrakuta dynasty made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deccan">Deccan</a> the centre of Indian power for two centuries, sending armies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and Kanchi, earning a place among the world’s four great kings in Arab accounts, carving the Kailasa temple at Ellora and sponsoring the first flowering of Kannada literature.</em></p>

